  "footnotes": [
    "9m37s YouTube explainer (`youtu.be/VQHRUQDCh_Q`), channel unnamed in the transcript. Register is hype-deflation: the thesis is that the headline is wrong on its face and interesting underneath it.",
    "The load-bearing reframe: JavaScript and WebAssembly are not rivals for one throne. \"JavaScript is the glue and the friendly face of the app. WebAssembly is the engine room where the heavy lifting quietly happens.\"",
    "Sourcing caveat — the numbers are asserted without citation: Blazor at ~42% of live Wasm, C++/Emscripten ~10%, Rust ~1.5%, 0.35% of all sites versus ~2% of the top thousand, sub-millisecond cold starts. Directionally consistent with public trackers; treat the specific figures as unverified.",
    "Two claims carry dates worth pinning: WebAssembly 3.0 shipping with built-in GC, and WASI 0.2 plus the component model stabilizing — both placed in 2025. Akamai's acquisition of Fermyon is placed in December 2025.",
    "ZP resonance (my framing, not the video's): KEEL §II.19 already names WASM modules implementing declared trait interfaces as the substrate's *single* composition primitive, and §V.1–V.3 spec host-import surfaces for Officer, Cartographer and Regent cores. The video's two 2025 developments land directly on that commitment — the component model is a stronger version of what the Extension Surface manifest is reaching for, and WASI 0.2 is the capability vocabulary `QUARANTINE-PLANE-2026-07.md` flags as an open position. The divergence: the video's sandbox is about running strangers' code fast, while ZP's is about proving who authorized a module to run at all. Same mechanism, different question."
